# 4. Detailed Audit

## 4.1 Attention Is All You Need

**Authors:** Ashish Vaswani et al.

**Year:** 2017

**Research Area:** Transformer architecture and self-attention.

**Relevance:** High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

This work provides foundational background for Transformer-based language models and self-attention mechanisms.

It is relevant because modern Large Language Models rely heavily on Transformer architectures for processing contextual information.

---

## 4.2 Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks

**Authors:** Patrick Lewis et al.

**Year:** 2020

**Research Area:** Retrieval-Augmented Generation.

**Relevance:** High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

This work is relevant to approaches that retrieve external information before generating an answer.

Retrieval-based approaches are important when studying alternatives to placing very large document collections directly into an LLM's context window.

---

## 4.3 RoFormer: Enhanced Transformer with Rotary Position Embedding

**Authors:** Jianlin Su et al.

**Year:** 2021

**Research Area:** Rotary Position Embedding.

**Relevance:** High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

Positional representation is important when studying long contexts because the location of information can influence model behavior.

This reference provides background on Rotary Position Embedding.

---

## 4.4 Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts

**Authors:** Nelson F. Liu et al.

**Year:** 2024

**Research Area:** Long-context information utilization.

**Relevance:** Very High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

This is a central reference for the research topic.

The study investigates how language models use information located at different positions within long contexts and demonstrates that model performance can vary depending on information position.

---

## 4.5 LongBench

**Authors:** Yushi Bai et al.

**Year:** 2024

**Research Area:** Long-context understanding.

**Relevance:** Very High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

LongBench provides a multitask benchmark for evaluating long-context understanding.

It is relevant for studying model performance on tasks such as question answering, summarization, and multi-document understanding.

---

## 4.6 RULER

**Authors:** Cheng-Ping Hsieh et al.

**Year:** 2024

**Research Area:** Long-context evaluation.

**Relevance:** Very High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

RULER provides a broader evaluation framework for long-context capabilities, including retrieval, multi-hop tasks, and aggregation.

It is useful for studying whether advertised context length corresponds to effective context utilization.

---

## 4.7 Efficient Streaming Language Models with Attention Sinks

**Authors:** Guangxuan Xiao et al.

**Year:** 2024

**Research Area:** Streaming language models and attention sinks.

**Relevance:** High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

This work investigates attention behavior in long or continuous sequences and proposes methods for efficient streaming language-model inference.

It provides relevant background for understanding long-sequence processing.

---

## 4.8 Needle in a Haystack

**Author:** Greg Kamradt

**Year:** 2023

**Research Area:** Long-context retrieval evaluation.

**Relevance:** High

**Audit Result:** Verified

### Reason for Inclusion

Needle-in-a-Haystack provides a practical method for testing whether a model can retrieve a specific piece of information from a large context.

The method is relevant to experiments involving evidence position and context length.

